1 NOTE Killed by the order of Francisco Pizarro.
2 CONT Atahualpa's reply to the Spaniard demand that he and his people convert to christianity.
2 CONT "Be advised that I, being free, do not have to pay tribute to anyone, nor do I believe there is a king greater than I, however, I will have the pleasure to be the friend of your emperor since he should be a great prince to send hi
2 CONC s armies throughout the world. But this pope does not interest me; much less will I obey him, I being in the kingdom of my father and our religion being good and I and my subjects are happy. Hpwever, despite my being a son of Huay
2 CONC na Capac, I cannot discuss anything so wise and old.
2 CONT The christ you speak of died, the Sun and Moon never die, besides, how do you know your god created the world?"
2 CONT This gave the Spanish the right to initiate the battle of Cajamarca, Nov 16, 1532. It was the beginning of the end of the great Inca empire. Several months later Atahualpa was tried in a mock trial and found guilty.
2 CONT he was sentenced to be burned. This horrified him because his belief was that a spirit that was burned cannot pass to his ancestors. He chose to be baptised in return for a more "honorable" death.
2 CONT he was baptised with the name, Juan Santos Atahualpa then he was strangled with a garrote.
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Cobo Bernabe, History of the New World, Bk 12.
2 CONT Source 2.
2 CONT Collection of documents unedited relative to the discovery, conquest and organization of the ancient Spanish possesions of ultramar. Altolaquine y Duvale, Bonilla, Madrid 1885-1932 Vol 15.
2 CONT Source 3
2 CONT Garcia de Castro, dispatch Lima, March 6, 1565, Governors of Peru letters and papers.
1 NOTE Died in smallpox epidemic brought by the Spaniards.
