1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Bower John Bower was born on 18th of March, 1795, in Washington County, near Hagerstown, Maryland. He was the eldest son of Barnhart and Mary Minnick Bower. John married Margaret Swinehart ca. 1818. A copy of 
2 CONC their marriage record is unavailable. Margaret was the daughter of Adam Swinehart, Sr. and Margaret Leslie. Adam Swinehart served in the Revolutionary War on the Indian frontier in the western part of Pennsylvania. He and his wife
2 CONC  Margaret came to Tuscarawas County, Ohio (now Carroll County) in the fall of 1816. John Bower purchased a farm in Carroll County, Ohio, in 1822 from his father Barnhart. John also owned several lots in Bowerston, which he sold to
2 CONC  the Steubenville and Indiana Railroad Company for the purpose of constructing a railroad station as well as a switch yard and side track in July 1854. John Bower's son Nathaniel Bower(s), along with John Bower daughter's husband 
2 CONC Henry Hoover helped to patent the town of Bowerston in 1851. John died September 7, 1863, in Bowerston. His wife, Margaret Swinehart Bower, died on June 23, 1863. They are both buried in the Longview Cemetery in Bowerston, Ohio. T
2 CONC he estate and probate records for John and Margaret Bower are missing from Carroll and Harrison County. John and Margaret Bower had ten children. After John Bower and his wife Margaret Swinehart Bower died, several of their childr
2 CONC en moved to Iowa. Nathaniel Bower(s), and Susannah Bower Hoover and her husband, Henry Hoover, settled in Johnson County, Iowa, in 1867. Henry Grimm and his wife, Catherine Bower, and Solomon Bower(s) and his family, settled in Bu
2 CONC chanan County, Iowa. Catherine Bower and her husband, Henry Grimm, moved later to Knox Township, Nebraska, where they founded the town of Grimton, Nebraska. Susannah Bower Hoover moved to Genoa, Nebraska, after the death of her hu
2 CONC sband in 1887. Solomon Bower(s) went to Nebraska, but he spent only a few years there. He returned to Wood County, Iowa, where he died.
2 CONT
