2 CONT Regimental History
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty-seventh Infantry IOWA
2 CONT
2 CONT (3 years)
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty-seventh Infantry. Col., James Gilbert Lieut.-Col., Jed
2 CONT
2 CONT Lake; Maj., George W. Howard.
2 CONT
2 CONT This regiment was organized in the summer of 1862 and was
2 CONT
2 CONT mustered in Oct. 3. A week later it reported to Maj.-Gen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope, commanding the Department of the Northwest, to take part
2 CONT
2 CONT in the campaign against the Indians, who were waging war
2 CONT
2 CONT against the settlers of Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon reaching St. Paul it went into quarters at Fort Snelling,
2 CONT
2 CONT and soon after Col. Gilbert, accompanied by six companies of
2 CONT
2 CONT the regiment, proceeded to Mille Lac, to superintend the
2 CONT
2 CONT payment of the annuity to the tribe at that point. In the
2 CONT
2 CONT meantime Maj. Howard had been ordered to report at Cairo, IL,
2 CONT
2 CONT with the remaining four companies and had departed for that
2 CONT
2 CONT place. Col. Gilbert received similar orders on his return
2 CONT
2 CONT from Mille Lac and moved at once.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment went into camp at Memphis but soon moved on the
2 CONT
2 CONT expedition into central Mississippi, marched to the
2 CONT
2 CONT Tallahatchie River, and was assigned to the duty of guarding
2 CONT
2 CONT the Mississippi Central railway between that point and the
2 CONT
2 CONT town of Waterford. In December a small band of cavalry made a
2 CONT
2 CONT dash on the regimental hospital, captured 11 men and hurried
2 CONT
2 CONT them across the country 15 miles, when, finding them unable to
2 CONT
2 CONT proceed, the officer in command paroled them and turned them
2 CONT
2 CONT loose.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment went to Jackson and marched with the other forces
2 CONT
2 CONT to assist Gen. Sullivan, who was in a fight with Forrest
2 CONT
2 CONT beyond Lexington, but failed to reach him in time to take part
2 CONT
2 CONT in the fray or even to overtake the flying enemy, who was in
2 CONT
2 CONT retreat for Clinton. It spent the winter at Jackson,
2 CONT
2 CONT suffering greatly from sickness, the result of the last
2 CONT
2 CONT campaign which was made without blankets or rations. The
2 CONT
2 CONT regiment held the post at Corinth part of April, and was then
2 CONT
2 CONT posted in detachments at various points along the railway from
2 CONT
2 CONT Jackson with Col. Gilbert in command of the post at that
2 CONT
2 CONT place.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June it moved to Lagrange, thence to Moscow, and performed
2 CONT
2 CONT similar duties near that place for two months, having frequent
2 CONT
2 CONT skirmishes with guerrillas. In August it formed part of a
2 CONT
2 CONT detached brigade which went to the support of Gen. Steele,
2 CONT
2 CONT then moving on Little Rock, and took part in the capture of
2 CONT
2 CONT that place. It remained there on guard and picket duty until
2 CONT
2 CONT Nov. 15, when it moved to Memphis and went into quarters until
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan. 26, 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT It then proceeded to Vicksburg and was assigned to the 2nd
2 CONT
2 CONT brigade, 3rd division, 16th corps, with which it took part in
2 CONT
2 CONT the Meridian raid and returned March 4. It accompanied the
2 CONT
2 CONT Red River expedition a few days later and was in numerous
2 CONT
2 CONT skirmishes and battles of that movement. At Pleasant Hill it
2 CONT
2 CONT lost 88 in killed and wounded, and it formed a part of the
2 CONT
2 CONT force that covered the retreat from Grand Ecore to Alexandria,
2 CONT
2 CONT almost constantly skirmishing on the way. It was also engaged
2 CONT
2 CONT at Marksville and Yellow bayou. It accompanied the forces
2 CONT
2 CONT under Smith, which dislodged Marmaduke from his position at
2 CONT
2 CONT Lake Chicot and then went into camp at Memphis.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was heavily engaged at Tupelo, and at the battle of Old
2 CONT
2 CONT Town Creek on the following day it took a prominent part.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to Memphis, it joined the Oxford expedition, after
2 CONT
2 CONT which it proceeded to Jefferson barracks, Mo. It accompanied
2 CONT
2 CONT an expedition through the state in the early part of October,
2 CONT
2 CONT in pursuit of Price, as far as Little Santa Fe on the Arkansas
2 CONT
2 CONT line, but returned without having had a fight.
2 CONT
2 CONT It accompanied Smith's forces to Nashville, where it took
2 CONT
2 CONT position in the line of defenses and was active in the battle
2 CONT
2 CONT in December. Col. Gilbert was in command of the brigade and
2 CONT
2 CONT its work was such as to win for him a commission as brigadier-
2 CONT
2 CONT general soon after. The regiment lost 61 but inflicted much
2 CONT
2 CONT greater damage on the enemy. In the pursuit it moved to
2 CONT
2 CONT Pulaski, thence to Clifton and Eastport and Feb. 9, embarked
2 CONT
2 CONT for Dauphin island, Ala.
2 CONT
2 CONT It took part in the operations about Mobile, aided in the
2 CONT
2 CONT siege of Fort Blakely, was in the final assault when its
2 CONT
2 CONT brigade captured 8 pieces of artillery and 600 prisoners.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gen. Gilbert was afterwards brevetted major-general for his
2 CONT
2 CONT skillful conduct in this affair. With its brigade the
2 CONT
2 CONT regiment was assigned duty in garrison work, but was soon
2 CONT
2 CONT after sent to Montgomery where it remained for two months and
2 CONT
2 CONT was mustered out in July, 1865, when its recruits were
2 CONT
2 CONT transferred to the veteran 12th IA
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The Union Army, vol. 4
2 CONT
