1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ❝ At 3 o'clock Monday morning, Jan. 14, she passed from life here to life eternal of the best women of Tuscarawas County—Mrs. Senia Lanning, widow of [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Lanning],
2 CONC  a former well known merchant of Gilmore, and mother of sons who also are well known in the commercial centers of this part of the state. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Lanning was a daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Gardner/6000000033099708923 David] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Gardner/6000000033099714039 Elizabeth Gardiner]. She was born in Greene County, Pennsy
2 CONC lvania, Jan. 17, 1820, and at her death had reached the age of eighty-five years and three days. Her parents came to Perry Township, Tuscarawas County, Ohio, when she was twelve years old. She became the wife of [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Lanning] May 9, 1847. He, too, was a resident of Perry Township. They moved to Gilmore the same year the village was laid out-1848. Mr. Lanning plowed out a lot and erected ther
2 CONC eon a building that served them for dwelling and store room for many years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The little store and dwelling gave way to something better as the years went by and the rewards of diligence and honesty came to them, but they continued to live in the same village for the rest of their lives, the husband passing
2 CONC  away in 1895. Their children came to them-all of whom are living-[https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870 Theodore Lanning], a prominent business man of Dennison; [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lanning
2 CONC /6000000202121634825 John H. Lanning], owner of a farm east of Gilmore; [https://www.geni.com/people/Normanda-Rosa-Nora-Lanning-Moore/6000000202120821831 Mrs. Normanda Moore], wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Moore/6000
2 CONC 000202120488858 Samuel Moore] of New York; Mrs. and [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Lanning/6000000202121584829 D. P. Lanning] director of one of the largest country stores in the county. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since his boyhood the writer has known this good woman, and it is but simple justice to say of her that she was one of God's noble women. She belonged to a generation that lacked the educational advantages of the present day, but 
2 CONC she had something far better than mere book knowledge-character. From her early childhood she was a Christian, becoming a member of the M. P. church and retaining membership therein until the day of her death. She was an earnest, 
2 CONC sincere and practical woman. She hated shame with a strength and intensity that could exist only in a strong and honest nature. She was no sentimentalist. But she was kind and helpful to the needy. She was ever ready to relieve wa
2 CONC nt, but she wanted people to do what they could to help themselves. She was decided in her convictions, but never intentionally unjust. Honest, energetic and upright herself, she had little patience with shiftlessness or meanness 
2 CONC or dishonesty in others. In this respect, as in many others, she exercised a noticeably good influence in the community. Her daily life was a lesson of thrift, of honesty, of clean living, of Christian duties well performed. Decep
2 CONC tion in any form was as foreign to her nature as day is to night. She loved the truth for its own sake and she hated a lie in any shape. To her husband she was a true helpmate; to her children she was a priceless blessing, for she
2 CONC  gave them character with their birth, and her stern and inflexible teaching led them in the right direction morally.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coming of the sturdy pioneer stock, Mrs. Lanning possessed physical as well as moral excellence.  She had good health through all her years till old age brought decadence. In February, 1906, she took to her bed. There did not seem
2 CONC  to be any specific disease-just a giving way of nature's forces. She suffered no pain. Last Wednesday a change came. From that time till death intervened she slowly sank. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services will be held Wednesday -probably at 1 o'clock-and interment will be in West Union cemetery where her husband was buried nearly twelve years ago. ❞
2 CONT
