1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==concerns==
2 CONT '''This Barnhart resembles https://www.geni.com/people/Barney-Bower/6000000000045389075 (shown here as a brother).  Are they supposed to be the same person?  Further research is warranted.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT *Barnhart Bower bought 600 acres of land from the Steubenville Land Company, built an upright sawmill and a grist mill in 1812. The work of the sawmill was carried on by his sons David and Jacob.  (...will add reference here, BWC)
2 CONT
2 CONT *Barnhart and Barnard seem to be used interchangably.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Used this source for date of death, burial location, confirmation and will.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~rwhitney/whitney/2/3802.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: June 1765, , Frederick Co., Maryland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Mary MINNICK about 1788 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 29 December 1844, Bowerston, Harrison Co., Ohio at age 79 3 
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried: 1844, Bowerston Cemetery, Harrison Co., Ohio 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Noted events in his life were:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Confirmation, 1779, , Frederick Co., Maryland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Will, 2 September 1843. 4 
2 CONT
2 CONT BOWERSTON (Monroe Twp.) Platted by David Bowers, Henry Hoover, Nathaniel Bower, Established August 21, 1852. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    In the early 1800s two crude mills were built on the south side of Conotton Creek; which was then practically a wilderness: One was a saw mill and the other was a grist mill: These mills-were, operated until the 1870's. The bui
2 CONC lder was Barnhard Bower. With his brother, John, and their families, the little caravan; had left Maryland in 1804. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    John and his family located on land owned later by Charles Crawford, half a mile north of Bowerston. Barnhard located in Salem for 5 years and then moved to what is now Bowerston. He started to build the mills in 1812. His home
2 CONC  was located above the mills on what is now Main Street. The father died and was the first to be buried in the cemetery C. B. 1810. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    John moved to Indiana, while Barnhard and his sons stayed, working the mills and sawing timber for people in the sparsely settled neighborhood. Bower had 4 boys and 3 girls. He deeded to two sons David and Jacob, the mills and 
2 CONC some lands in 1827. To other sons he gave a farm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    The pioneer settlement, laid out in 1816, was first called Bower's Mills and later Bowersville. In 1883, it was changed to Bowerston. Since then, the last "w" was dropped. Bowers operated the two mills until the 1850's and then
2 CONC  sold them to Peter and Joseph Overholt who rebuilt them. Since then, various owners have been the Orr boys, Billy Roof, Stewart Price, and Molesworth. In 1937, the old mill was torn down. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Barnhart Bower:
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart Bower, was born June 1765, in Frederick County, Maryland. He was the son of Conrad and Eva Bower.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart married Mary Minnick. She was the daughter of Henry and Catherine Minnick. Henry Minnick died in
2 CONT
2 CONT 1805 in Franklin County, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart and his wife Mary lived in Washington County, near Hagerstown, Maryland. They moved to Jefferson
2 CONT
2 CONT County, Ohio in 1805. When they arrived, they stayed with Barnhart Bower's brother and sister, who settled there
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1800. His brother and sisters were members of the Bower and Easterday families who settled in Redstone,
2 CONT
2 CONT Pennsylvania on the Monongahela River in 1796. In April of 1800, George Bower and Martin Easterday II went
2 CONT
2 CONT by raft Down the Monongahela and Ohio rivers to Know Township in Jefferson County, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart purchased 160 acres of land at the Steubenville, Ohio Land office on 7th of November, 1805, located in
2 CONT
2 CONT the R6, T13, SW Q S28, located about a mile north the present town of Bowerston. On 17th of November, 1808,
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart purchased a 159 acre tract of land where Bowerston, Ohio now stands. In1812, Barnhart built a gristmill and a sawmill. The power to operate the mills came from One Leg Creek, which was located on the
2 CONT
2 CONT property. Barnhart built a log cabin and lived a short distance from the mills. This was the beginning of
2 CONT
2 CONT Bowerston, first known as Bowers Mill, then as Bowerville. The name was changed to Bowerston in 1883. The
2 CONT
2 CONT town was patented on 22nd of March, 1851, by Barnhart Bower's son David, and his grandson Nathaniel
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower(s) and his granddaughter's husband Henry Hoover.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart deeded the mills to his sons, David and Jacob. He deeded farm land to his sons John and Henry in
2 CONT
2 CONT Harrison County. He deeded his farm land near Steubenville to his son George.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart died 29th of December, 1843, at age 84. Mary Minnick Bower died 6th of May, 1847, at age 81.
2 CONT
2 CONT They are both buried at the Longview Cemetery in Bowerston, Ohio. Barnhart and Mary Bower had nine
2 CONT
2 CONT children.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About Barnhart Bower:
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Longview Cemetery, Bowerston, Ohio Section B
2 CONT
