2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Conrad is the immigrant ancestor of the Bower family who foundedBowerston,Ohio. Early Lutheran Church records in Bowerstonstate that Conrad arrived from Saxony, Germany on the ship"Neptune" with Captain John Mason. It sailed
2 CONC  from RotterdaytoPhiladelphia and arrived on Tuesday, September 24, 1751. Hetook the Oath of Allegiance when he arrived, and settled firstin Frederick County, Maryland. Arlene F. Mansfield did theresearch on this family, wirting t
2 CONC he book "Decendants of theBower, Easterday, Hoover and Swinehart Families". Evidence ofConrad's first land , purchase in Frederick County, MD, isrecorded in Book L. Page 597 on Dec. 24, 1768. It was locatedwest o fMiddletown, not 
2 CONC for from Boonsboro. In 1790 he wasliving in Washington County, MD, then listed in Frederick Countyin the 1800 census. Church records spell his name Bauer, in theGerman fashion. The Maryland Land Records use the Englishspelling Bow
2 CONC er.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before he died, Conrad settled, along with several of hischildren, south of New Hagerstown on the north side of ConottonCreet in what jbecame Carroll County, Ohio. At the time thearea wa part of One Leg Township in Tuscarawas Coun
2 CONC ty, Ohio.Conrad was the first one buried in the old Lutheran Cemetery inBowerston. The cemetery is located on land once owned by JohnBower and Jacob Easterday, and it was renamed Longview LuthranCemetery in Bowerston. According to
2 CONC  a historical sectionreported in the Cadiz REPUBLICAN newspapter in 1940, Conrad'sgravestone is laid flat on the ground, and can be found only bythose who kno its exact location. Crudely carved int ehnativestone are the initials C
2 CONC .B. and the;year 1810.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence of his children was found in the Federick EvangelicalLutheran Church records in Frederick county, Maryland. Three of;his daughters married sons of Martin Easterday, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I18 
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Margaret BOWER 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sex: F 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1846 in ,Harrison,OH 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 1850 Census - Harrison Co., Ohio Monroe Township 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Nathaniel BOWER b: 29 JUL 1824 in ,Harrison,OH 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Esther WOOD b: ABT 1827
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Marie Eva
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Mary MINNICH b: 1766 in ,,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 1788
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT  BOWER b: ABT 1789 in ,,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  Magdelena Mary BOWER b: 4 MAY 1791 in Hagerstown,Washington,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  BOWER b: ABT 1792
2 CONT
2 CONT  John BOWERS b: 18 MAR 1795 in Hagerstown,Washington,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  Catherine BOWER b: 1798 in Hagerstown,Washington,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jacob Sr. BOWER b: 18 JAN 1801 in Hagerstown,Washington,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  David BOWER b: 14 MAY 1803 in ,Washington,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  George BOWER b: 1783 in ,,MD
2 CONT
2 CONT  Henry BOWER b: 19 MAY 1806 in ,Jefferson,OH
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1806 Barnhart and his wife and children: George, John, David, Henry,
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob and daughter Catherine along with Barnhart's brother John and his family
2 CONT
2 CONT left Maryland for Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known which route the Bower family took from Maryland to Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT They most likely traveled some of the distance on the National Road from
2 CONT
2 CONT Maryland across West Virginia through the County, Ohio or they could of travele
2 CONT
2 CONT part ofthe distance by boat down the Ohio River finally going ashore at
2 CONT
2 CONT Steubenville, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been reported that Barnhart Bower and his family settled near salem
2 CONT
2 CONT Ohio and lived there for five years before moving to a place on the south side
2 CONT
2 CONT of Conotton Creek near the present town of Bowerston in Harrison County, Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said Barnhart's father C. Bower established the settlement of New
2 CONT
2 CONT Hagerstown, Ohio located on the north side of Conotton Creek and that they had
2 CONT
2 CONT to cut a wagon road through from Jewett to their new settlements. Barnhart
2 CONT
2 CONT built the first flour mill in this area in 1812. He built his home on a hill
2 CONT
2 CONT above the mill on what is now Main Street. His brogher John built the first
2 CONT
2 CONT saw mill in the area. This pioneer settlement was laid out in 1816. It was
2 CONT
2 CONT first called Bower's Mills and later Bowersville. The town was patent by Henry
2 CONT
2 CONT Hoover, David Bower and Nathaniel Bower on Mar. 22, 1851. In 1883 the name of
2 CONT
2 CONT the town was changed to Bowerston. Barnhart's brother John moved to Indiana,
2 CONT
2 CONT while Barnhart and his sons stayed, working the flour mill and sawing timber
2 CONT
2 CONT for people in the sparsly neighborhood. These two mills continued to operate
2 CONT
2 CONT until the 1870's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart and his wife Mary had 5 sons and possible 3 daughters. In 1827
2 CONT
2 CONT Barnhart deeded the two mills and some land to his two sons, David JP and Jacob
2 CONT
2 CONT Sr. He deeded some farm land in the Bowerston area to his tow sons, John and
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry. His son George was the only one that did not live near Bowerston.
2 CONT
2 CONT George lived near Steubenville, Ohio. The Topes family claim that George
2 CONT
2 CONT stayed near Salem and farmed the land that Barnhart Bower had there. Barnhart
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower's will was dated September 2, 1843. It was probated on January 18, 1845.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower died on May 6, 1847 at the age of 81. Both are buried at Longview
2 CONT
2 CONT Cemetery, Bowerston, Ohio. Barnhart Bower's father C. Bower was the first
2 CONT
2 CONT member of the family buried in this Cemetery. The Longview Cemetery was named
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lutheran Cemetery. The cemetery has many old tombstones bearing the name
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Barnhart Bower Will
2 CONT
2 CONT New Heral Dec. 30,1976
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes by Ambrose M. Bower in 1935. R. Halsted from Hubbard, Iowa
2 CONT
2 CONT Compiled: by Arlene F. Mansfield, 498 Quartz St., Los Alamos, NM 87544
2 CONT
2 CONT BERNARD BOWER WILL
2 CONT
2 CONT Know all men by these presents, that I Bernard Bower of the county of
2 CONT
2 CONT Harrison
2 CONT
2 CONT and State of Hoio being weak in body but of sound mind and memory, do make and
2 CONT
2 CONT publish this my las will and Testament - First it is my will that all my just
2 CONT
2 CONT debts be fully paid-
2 CONT
2 CONT Second, I give and bequeath to my beloved wife Mary all the Household and
2 CONT
2 CONT Kitchen furniture as long as she shall live and all the money that shall be on
2 CONT
2 CONT hand at my decease. She is to have the use of during her life - and after her
2 CONT
2 CONT decease the money that shall be on hand, if any shall be divided equally among
2 CONT
2 CONT my children share and share alike - And also it is my will, that my beloved
2 CONT
2 CONT wife Mary shall have the house that we now live in, for her to live in as long
2 CONT
2 CONT as she shall live and also the use of the garden, or so much of it as she shall
2 CONT
2 CONT wish to occupy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thirdly, I do give and bequeath to Ezekiel (listed Hesekiah in the census
2 CONT
2 CONT of 1850 in Harrison Co, Ohio, Monroe Township) Bower, Son of my son David Bower
2 CONT
2 CONT the sum of fifty dollars in money --
2 CONT
2 CONT Fourthly, I do give and bequeath to Bernard Bower, son of my son John
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower, the sum of fifty dollars in money -
2 CONT
2 CONT Fifthly, I do give and bequeath for the sue of the Lutheran Meeting House
2 CONT
2 CONT and grave yard, the sum of fifty dollars in money
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixthly, I do give and bequeath to my daughter Matlena Gundy two hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT dollars in money, and after the decease of my beloved wife Mary, all the
2 CONT
2 CONT Household and Kitchen funriture, except the stove is to be equally divided
2 CONT
2 CONT between her and her sister Catherine Crumrine --
2 CONT
2 CONT Seventhly, I do give and bequeath to my daughter Catherine Crumrine, the
2 CONT
2 CONT sum of one hundred dollars in money --
2 CONT
2 CONT Eighthly, I so give and bequeath to my son Henry Bower the Stove that I no
2 CONT
2 CONT have --
2 CONT
2 CONT Ninthly, I do give and bequeath to my son John Bower the sum of ten dollar
2 CONT
2 CONT in money
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenthly, I do give and bequeath to my son Jacob Bower, the sum of ten
2 CONT
2 CONT dollars in money --
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleventhly, I do give and bequeath to my son David Bower the sum of ten
2 CONT
2 CONT dollars in money --
2 CONT
2 CONT And Lastly I do hereby constitute and appoint my sons Jacob Bower and Davi
2 CONT
2 CONT Bower Executors of this my (will).
2 CONT
2 CONT (photocopy received from Arlene Mansfield - 1984)
2 CONT
