2 CONT
2 CONT infancy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David Bower, grandfather of Charles A. Bower, was born in Washington County, Maryland, and reared in Monroe Township. As he was growing up he frequently played with Indian children belonging to friendly tribes, and found them much
2 CONC  the same as white ones. In 1856 he was one of the men who laid out the village of Bowerston, and he was engaged in farming and milling all of his life, owning a large amount of land and being a man of importance. For a number of 
2 CONC years he served as a justice of the peace. He married Elizabeth Swinehart, and they had the following children: Jeremiah, Iziah, Ezekiel, Obediab, William, John A., David T., Mary A., Caroline and Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.heritagepursuit.com/Tuscarawas/TuscarawasBWarren.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN A. BOWERS, merchant, New Cumberland, was born September 1, 1847, in Bowerston, Harrison Co., Ohio, and is a son of David and Elizabeth (Swinehart) Bowers. His father was an early settler of Harrison County, and laid out the t
2 CONC own of Bowerston. He died in 1857. Our subject's mother died in 1867. Mr. Bowers was the first merchant in Monroe Township, Carroll County. His family numbered eleven children; viz., an infant, deceased ; Jeremiah, living in Wood 
2 CONC County, Ohio ; Mary Ann, wife of Peter McCurdy, of Dennison, Ohio ; Isaiah. a physician of Plymouth, Ind. ; Ezekiel. now living at Bowerston ; Margaret, deceased ; Obadiah. deceased ; Caroline, wife of Dr. J. B. McCombs, of Iowa; 
2 CONC William H., who was starved to death in Libby Prison : John A. and David T., now farming near Bowerston. At sixteen, our subject began learning the boot and shoe trade. which he followed about seventeen rears, He was united in mar
2 CONC riage. July 4, 1877, with Miss Elizabeth Tressel, daughter of Daniel and Abigail Tressel. (See their biography.) He then opened a general store in New Cumberland, in which he is still engaged. To Mr. and Mrs. Bowers was born one c
2 CONC hild- Myrtle M. A. Our subject, is an advocate of the principles of the National Greenback party, He and his wife are members of the Lutheran Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The village of Bowerston was settled in the early 1800’s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two crude mills were built on the south side of
2 CONT
2 CONT Conotton Creek, which was then practically a
2 CONT
2 CONT wilderness. One mill was a sawmill, the other a
2 CONT
2 CONT gristmill. In 1804, Barnhard Bower and his brother John
2 CONT
2 CONT moved to the area and started to rebuild the mills. In
2 CONT
2 CONT 1816, the settlement called Bower's Mills was laid out.
2 CONT
2 CONT The village was first called Bowers Mill, then
2 CONT
2 CONT Bowersville, then Bowerstown, and finally Bowerston.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name was changed from Bowerstown to Bowerston
2 CONT
2 CONT because there was another town in Ohio by the name of
2 CONT
2 CONT Bowerstown. Bowerston was platted by David Bower,
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Hoover, and Nathaniel Bower on August 21, 1851
2 CONT
2 CONT and was filed in court August 2, 1852. Incorporation
2 CONT
2 CONT followed in 1881.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.bowerstonlibrary.org/Bow_history/coverpghist1.htm
