2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Hoover
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 	28 Jun 1818
2 CONT
2 CONT [city], Jefferson, Ohio, USA  	
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 	2 Sep 1887
2 CONT
2 CONT Hardin Township, Johnson, Iowa, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse  	  
2 CONT
2 CONT Susannah Bowers
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 13 Feb 1825 in [city], Carroll, Ohio, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 14 Nov 1907 in Genona, Nance, Nebraska, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: 14 Dec 1843 in [city], Carroll, Ohio, USA 	 View Info
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	Sex 	Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT John Bower Hoover 	M 	12 Aug 1844 in Bowerston, [county], Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Ann Hoover 	F 	abt 1847 in Harrison, [county], Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT David Calvin Hoover 	M 	30 Aug 1849 in [city], Harrison, Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Hoover 	M 	22 Apr 1854 in Harrison, Hamilton, Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Hoover 	M 	23 Aug 1856 in [city], Harrison, Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel B Hoover 	M 	18 Mar 1864 in [city], Harrison, Ohio, USA 	 
2 CONT
2 CONT James S Hoover 	M 	9 Mar 1873 in [city], Johnson, Iowa, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BOWERSTON (Monroe Twp.) Platted by David Bowers, Henry Hoover, Nathaniel Bower, Established August 21, 1852. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    In the early 1800s two crude mills were built on the south side of Conotton Creek; which was then practically a wilderness: One was a saw mill and the other was a grist mill: These mills-were, operated until the 1870's. The bui
2 CONC lder was Barnhard Bower. With his brother, John, and their families, the little caravan; had left Maryland in 1804. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    John and his family located on land owned later by Charles Crawford, half a mile north of Bowerston. Barnhard located in Salem for 5 years and then moved to what is now Bowerston. He started to build the mills in 1812. His home
2 CONC  was located above the mills on what is now Main Street. The father died and was the first to be buried in the cemetery C. B. 1810. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    John moved to Indiana, while Barnhard and his sons stayed, working the mills and sawing timber for people in the sparsely settled neighborhood. Bower had 4 boys and 3 girls. He deeded to two sons David and Jacob, the mills and 
2 CONC some lands in 1827. To other sons he gave a farm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    The pioneer settlement, laid out in 1816, was first called Bower's Mills and later Bowersville. In 1883, it was changed to Bowerston. Since then, the last "w" was dropped. Bowers operated the two mills until the 1850's and then
2 CONC  sold them to Peter and Joseph Overholt who rebuilt them. Since then, various owners have been the Orr boys, Billy Roof, Stewart Price, and Molesworth. In 1937, the old mill was torn down. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Bower(s), and Susannah Bower Hoover and her husband, Henry Hoover, settled in Johnson County,
2 CONT
2 CONT Iowa, in 1867.
2 CONT
