1 NAME Philip /Lanning/
2 GIVN Philip
2 SURN Lanning
2 _MARNM Lanning
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC West Union Cemetery 
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 13679 Gilmore Road Southeast
3 CITY Port Washington
3 STAE OH
3 POST 43837
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 JAN 1821
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 old Coshocton Road
3 CITY near Newtown
3 STAE OH
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 AUG 1895
2 ADDR
3 CITY Gilmore
3 STAE OH
3 POST 43837
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Merchant, Post Master
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ❝ He was the Oldest Postmaster and One of the Best Known and Most Reputable Business Men In the County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Saturday morning, August, 17, 1885, ten minutes after Friday's midnight hour had passed, [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Laning], who had been continuously in business in Tuscarawas county fo
2 CONC r nearly a half century, died at his home in Gilmore, after an illness of 4 months and 2 days. Mr. Laning's life was such that his death was not unexpected by the ordinary newspaper editor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born about a mile west of the village of New Concord, (before the village had an "official" name), on what is known as the Coshocton road; January 24, 1821. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He got such an education as the backwoods schools gave, but had an equipment that text books and teachers did not furnish honesty, energy, ambition, tact, taste and shrewd common sense. 
2 CONT
2 CONT May 9, 1837, he married [https://www.geni.com/people/Senia-Lanning/6000000202121137911?through=6000000162398431821 Acenith Gardner], a "young girl" who, like himself, had a large stock of character and good sense. In his early bus
2 CONC iness struggles she was his thrifty helper; when prosperity came she was none the less a blessing, and in the hours of his last illness her presence gave him comfort when nothing but faithfulness - tenderness and love could yield 
2 CONC him consolation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To them were born four children: all living —[https://www.geni.com/people/Normanda-Rosa-Nora-Lanning-Moore/6000000202120821831?through=6000000202120488858 Mrs. Normandy Moore], of San Francisco, Cal., who with her son, [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/John-Moore/6000000202122214873?through=6000000202120821831 John], was at the bedside of her dying father; [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870?through=6000000202121137911 Theodore], a
2 CONC  leading merchant, of Dennison; [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lanning/6000000202121634825?through=6000000202121422870 John H.], a farmer, residing between Gilmore and Westchester, and [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Lanning
2 CONC /6000000202121584829?through=6000000202120821831  D. P. Laning], who for some time as partner has managed the large mercantile business of P. Laning & Son at Gilmore.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000225394649843&size=large}} {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000225394526893&size=large}}
2 CONT Mr. Laning's business career is an interesting one, for it shows what energy and capacity will accomplish. He bought the second lot sold in the newly said out town of Gilmore, and walked three miles from his home every morning to 
2 CONC grub and clear the lot and excavate for a cellar. He was at his work before sunrise each morning, brought his dinner with him, and worked as long as he could see in the evening before starting on the home-ward walk. The house he b
2 CONC uilt was (1833). There was a partition in the middle, making two rooms of 15x16 each. Mr. and Mrs. Laning lived in the rear room, while the front was occupied by a stock of goods bought for $140, the proceeds of the sale of a hors
2 CONC e and buggy. He paid close attention to business, but-by-enlarging his stock he got somewhat in debt. The first year he lost $221, on butter sent to Wheeling. This made him quite worse about morning, and when he heard, then he was
2 CONC  much worried, nor kept the matter to himself. That night, after closing the store, he walked up the street of the little village, and suggested a market. He resolved to open his quarter-board. The idea soon ripened, however, and 
2 CONC before he took any definite steps, he died. As the years went by the business grew. 
2 CONT The little 15x16 store room was exchanged for a large new building. This was afterward enlarged by building an extension. Later another large building, was erected with improved arrangements inside. 
2 CONT For many years the Laning store has been considered the finest, best arranged country store in eastern Ohio, while the amount of business transacted has been very large. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His oldest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870 Theodore], was for many years a partner and an active factor in connection with his father's business, and since his death has continued in building
2 CONC  up the business. For saturated years past his youngest son, [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Lanning/6000000202121584829 D. P.], has been a partner and has taken a prominent part in managing the affairs of the firm. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Mr. Laning's distinguishing traits were honorable dealing and a charitable disposition. He was benevolent to a large degree. No depriving needy one ever asked his help in vain, and many a poor family has had reason to hold h
2 CONC im in grateful remem- brance. No man in the county has given presents (favor) to a larger number of people. While he was large- hearted, he was not the more paralyzed by generosity. Many of his kind acts were known only to himself
2 CONC  and the recipients. The writer's life-long acquaintance with the deceased gives him knowledge of this noble trait in Mr. Laning's character. Many were the kindnesses we received from him when a boy, and never can we forget him fo
2 CONC r his helpful interest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For two generations [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Laning] was the leading figure in the village. In every home for miles around his name was a household word, and it was a synonym for uprig
2 CONC ht dealing and clean, honorable life. At the time of his death he was the oldest postmaster in the county, having been appointed, we believe, in 1858 or '59, holding the office an unbroken chain since through all the political cha
2 CONC nges. In politics he was a Democrat. Mr. Laning helped organize the Methodist Protestant church in Gilmore, and has ever been its chief financial supporter. The church will miss him as well may the community in general. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held Sunday afternoon, the remains being buried in West Union cemetery. The attendance was larger than ever before seen at a funeral in that section. The line of carriages extended from town almost to the cemetery,
2 CONC  a mile away, while at the church fully half as many vehicles were waiting. People came from far and near. They were there from New Conwentown, Port Washington, Gosdenhanten, Oh; Richville, Dennison, Tippcconoc; New Philadelphia a
2 CONC nd many other places. The church could not contain the crowd. Short, impressive addresses were made by Rev. H. M. Bradley and Rev. David Pierce. When the audience was given an opportunity to view the remains the people marched by 
2 CONC the casket in solemn procession for a last look at one they loved, and many were seen to reverently touch the forehead or hands of their dead friend as they marched by. All classes were represented-the rich and poor, the old, youn
2 CONC g and middle-aged. Little children went by, and some of them were lifted up that they might see the face of one who had been so long honored in the community. It was remarkable scene, and an object lesson to the young, but no more
2 CONC  so than the life of the departed had been. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Lanning]'s life was an object lesson full of helpful suggestions to every young man who is trying to make his way in the world. Though his career is finished t
2 CONC he influence of his example will long remain to point the way to honorable success.  ❞
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''“The Gardners of Perry Township, Tuscarawas County, Ohio”, by Charles F. Gardner, 1899- ; Page 62 (http://www.familysearch.org/library/books/idviewer/328574/168) {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224850859821&
2 CONC size=large}} Philip’s Obituary Clipping + Unknown Clipping
2 CONT 4em>''… Part 2 (unknown clipping) .…: ''
2 CONT
2 CONT ❝  [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 PHILIP LANNING], merchant and Postmaster at Gilmore, was born in Washington Township, Tuscarawas Co., Ohio, in 1821. He is the second son
2 CONT of [https://www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Lanning/6000000162186119821 Joseph] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Phoebe-Lanning/6000000162397046849 Elizabeth Lanning], who emigrated to this county in 1818. [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Joseph-Lanning/6000000162186119821 Joseph Lanning] was a native of New Jersey, and died in this county in 1878, at the ripe old age of eighty-one years. His wife is still living, and makes her home with her daughter, [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/Sarah-Denning/6000000162398436821 Sarah A.], wife of William Denning, of this township. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 Philip Lanning] was raised in Tuscarawas County, and received only a limited education. When about twenty-four years of age, he started out in life for himself. In 18
2 CONC 47, he was married to [https://www.geni.com/people/Senia-Lanning/6000000202121137911 Senia Gardner], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Gardner/6000000033099708923 David Gardner], who came here from Pennsylvania in 183
2 CONC 4. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our subject moved to Gilmore, his present place of business, in 1848, and entered the mercantile trade. He first built a small store-room, and lived in a part of the same for a number of years. He hauled his goods from Wheeling, W
2 CONC . Va., a distance of forty-five miles. About this time he sold a horse and buggy, which furnished him money to start in business. In 1861, he built a large store room, where he is now carrying on an immense mercantile trade. The p
2 CONC ost office is located in his building.
2 CONT
2 CONT Four children have been born to him-- [https://www.geni.com/people/Normanda-Rosa-Nora-Lanning-Moore/6000000202120821831 Nora Manda], wife of [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Moore/6000000202120488858 S. G. Moore], of Galva, Ill
2 CONC .;  [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870 Theodore], who was married in 1874, to Miss Nannie Mears, of Westchester, Ohio, and is now in partnership with his father;  [https://www.geni.com/people/John-La
2 CONC nning/6000000202121634825 John H.], who was married in 1878, to Miss Appie Billingsley, of Columbiana County;  and [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Lanning/6000000202121584829 David P.], who is now living in Illinois. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Both our subject and wife are members of the Methodist Protestant Church.  Mr. Lanning is also a member of the Masonic fraternity. His son, [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870 Theodore], is a member o
2 CONC f the I.O.O.F. He has been quite prosperous in business, although about two years ago their store and safe were burglarize, and they sustained a loss of $1,000. They have also, from time to time, sustained heavy losses in differen
2 CONC t ways. He is now the owner of 130 acres of highly improved land, with new buildings on the same, besides a large amount of village property, including a heavy stock of goods. He is one of the first men of Washington Township. ❞
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '' 1875, “History of Tuscarawas County, Ohio Combination atlas map of Tuscarawas County, Ohio”, L. H. Pub.1875; Everts, Strasburg, Ohio: Gordon Print., 359 pgs.; Biographies: Theodore Lanning (https://ohiogenealogyexpress.com/tusc
2 CONC arawas/TuscarawasCo_Hist_1875_bios/Tuscarawas1875_bios_l.html) '' 
2 CONT
2 CONT .. ❝  [https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Lanning-Jr/6000000206448775836 Richard LANNING] was born March 12, 1763, in New Jersey. After living for some time in Allegany County, Maryland, he came about 1801, to Jefferson County, O
2 CONC hio, where he died February 26, 1844.  His sons were [https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Lanning-Jr/6000000079634198047 Richard],[https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162186129821 Philip], [https://www.geni.com/peopl
2 CONC e/Isaac-Lanning/6000000162185930822 Isaac], [https://www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Lanning/6000000162186119821 Joseph], [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lanning/6000000204241098823  John], Jacob and [https://www.geni.com/people/Abra
2 CONC ham-Lanning/6000000140346414832?through=6000000162186119821 Abram], and his daughters were [https://www.geni.com/people/Hannah-Parkinson/6000000162186130821 Hannah], [https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Meek/6000000162186113821 Mary]
2 CONC , [https://www.geni.com/people/Nancy-Severson/6000000162186110821 Nancy] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Rachel-Meek/6000000162186109821 Rachel].  One of these daughters married a Parkinson and another a Meek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his sons, [https://www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Lanning/6000000162186119821 Joseph] was born in Allegheny County, November 30, 1734, and came with his father to Ohio.  He married [https://www.geni.com/people/Phoebe-Lanning/600000
2 CONC 0162397046849 Elizabeth Holmes], Daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Holmes/6000000003615144858 Samuel Holmes].
2 CONT In 1818 he moved to a farm on the old Coshocton road near Newtown, where four of his sons were born: [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Lanning/6000000162398256832  Samuel], [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/60000001623
2 CONC 98431821 '''Philip''], [https://www.geni.com/people/Isaac-Lanning/6000000162398425821 Isaac] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Maj-Pvt-Richard-Lanning/6000000113550887843 Richard]. In 1827 he moved to Perry Township on the Gilmore 
2 CONC and West Chester road, where his grandson, [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lanning/6000000202121634825 John H. Lanning], now resides.  At this place were born his other children: [https://www.geni.com/people/Martin-Van-Buren-Lan
2 CONC ning/6000000162398193826?through=6000000162398436821 Martin Van Burea], [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Lanning/6000000162398437821 John], [https://www.geni.com/people/Sarah-Denning/6000000162398436821 Sarah Ann] and [https://ww
2 CONC w.geni.com/people/Mary-Cissell/6000000162398424821 Mary Elizabeth].
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 '''Philip'''], the second son, born January 24, 1821, helped his father in clearing the farm till twenty-eight years old. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 9, 1847, [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 '''Philip'''] married [https://www.geni.com/people/Senia-Lanning/6000000202121137911 Senia], daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/David-Gardner/60
2 CONC 00000033099708923 David] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Gardner/6000000033099714039 Elizabeth Gardner], who came to Perry Township in 1830 from Western Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the founding of Gilmore in 1848, [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 '''Philip Lanning''' ]bought the second lot sold, and at once cleared the ground and dug the colar, during which he walked three a
2 CONC nd one half miles night and morning to his father's house where he still lived.
2 CONT He had sold a horse and buggy for $128.00 which was his entire capital. A one-story building vighteen by thirty-six feet with a partition in the center was his home and store until 1860, where he put up a commodinus two story buil
2 CONC ding on the adjoining lot.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the beginning of the present extensive mercantile business of the Lannings.
2 CONT At one time in the strife for trade with toro competitors, butter was advanced from 8 cents to 28 cents per pound. When his stock was sold at Wheeling [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821 '''Philip Lanni
2 CONC ng'''] found that he was in debt $500.00 more than he was worth.
2 CONT
2 CONT After walking the street nearly all night, he decided to say nothing about his loss and go ahead.
2 CONT It was five years befure he even told his wife. In the mean time his competitors were out of business and he was safe, and prospered until his death, August 17, 1895.
2 CONT
