1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ❝ 𝐀 𝐖𝐎𝐌𝐀𝐍 𝐎𝐅 𝐖𝐎𝐑𝐓𝐇 𝐖𝐀𝐒 𝐂𝐀𝐋𝐋𝐄𝐃 𝐖𝐇𝐄𝐍 𝐍𝐎𝐑𝐀 𝐋𝐀𝐍𝐍𝐈𝐍𝐆 𝐌𝐎𝐎𝐑𝐄, 𝐀 𝐆𝐈𝐋𝐌𝐎𝐑𝐄 𝐆𝐈𝐑𝐋, 𝐖𝐄𝐍𝐓 𝐇𝐎𝐌𝐄
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Gilmore, Ohio. lived in various parts of the West, died in New York City, a woman of unusual business ability, useful and loved in her home, respected by her acquaintances. This sums up the history of Mrs. Nora Lanning Moo
2 CONC re who died Sept. 26, 1919.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nora R. Lanning, daughter of [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-Lanning/6000000162398431821?through=6000000202120821831 Philip] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Senia-Lanning/6000000202121137911?through=6000000162398431821 Senia 
2 CONC Gardner Lanning], was born in Gilmore, Tuscarawas county, Ohio, April 20, 1849. Her father and mother Were natives of Tuscarawas county, and the father was the first store-keeper, in Gilmore, building the second house in that vill
2 CONC age after the place had been platted by its founder, Walter M. Blake, the first sheriff of Tuscarawas county. For many years Mr. and Mrs. Lanning continued to live in Gilmore, he being in the mercantile business until his death in
2 CONC  1895. Both were among the most highly esteemed and respected citizens of the county. Their virtues are fresh in the memories of those living, who knew them, to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nora was the second child born in the village, Harry Bates, son of Mr. and Mrs. John Bates, being the first.
2 CONT
2 CONT A bright and beautiful girl, the belle of the community, she attracted the attention and won the love of [https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-Moore/6000000202120488858?through=6000000203539784846 Samuel G. Moore], of near Fairview,
2 CONC  Guernsey county who visited the village on business, and they were married February 14, 1867, just before she was 18 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Moore saw a chance for business development in the West, and they moved to Robinson, Kansas, in August, 1869. They remained there until 1874 when they moved to Seneca, Kansas, which was their home until 1888 when they
2 CONC  went to Galva, Ills., where they resided for about a year. In 1884 they went to Ottawa, Kansas, and three years later moved to Wichita, Kansas. They left Wichita in 1890 for Denver Colorado, where they remained until 1893 when th
2 CONC ey located in San Francisco, California. In 1895 they went to New York City where they continued to reside until Mrs. Moore's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the 26 years they were in the West, about three were passed on a farm.{{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224833865892&size=large}}The rest of the time they were engaged in mercantile, laundry business and toilet
2 CONC  soap manufacturing. {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224833786900&size=large}}When changes in location or business were contemplated Mrs. Moore was always consulted. In fact she was the moring spirit and guiding
2 CONC  hand in all that was done in this line. Her judgment was good, her intuitions were keen, and she was an adviser her husband appreciated.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224842900831&size=large}}
2 CONT Of the family, four children and the husband survive. One child, [https://www.geni.com/people/Harry-Moore/6000000203539784846?through=6000000139240513845 Harry], died in infancy. The children are—
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Moore/6000000202122214873?through=6000000202120821831 John L]…. [https://www.geni.com/people/Roy-E-Moore-the-Father-of-American-Gymnastics/6000000202120989852?through=6000000202120703876 Roy E.]… 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Dr-Earl-Moore-Sr/6000000139240513845?through=6000000202120989852 Earl T. ]and [https://www.geni.com/people/Mabel-Effie-Moore-Hearon/6000000202120703876?through=6000000202120821831 Mrs. Mabel Herron] ''
2 CONC [sic, Hearon]'' of Cranford, N. J.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since going to New York the husband and sons have been engaged in the real estate business and manufacturing toilet soap, soap powder, etc. They have, besides their soap factory, four apartment houses. two in New York City and two
2 CONC  in Binghamton, N. Y. These four houses have 94 separate flats. Three of these houses are furnished, and the fourth is just finished but not furnished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Moore's health had been good until within the past six or seven months, yet during all of that time, until within two weeks of her death, she had charge of one of the apartment houses which was a home for 20 families. The car
2 CONC es and worry incident to this work no doubt hastened her death. Her husband and children wanted her to give up the work last spring, but could not persuade her to do so. Her indomitable spirit kept her going until the breakdown ca
2 CONC me.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was conscious until the end. Her last words were to her husband, about 10 Thursday evening, when she said: ''"Oh Pa !"'' as he approached her bedside. She then went to sleep and passed away at 2 o'clock Friday morning, Sept. 2
2 CONC 6. Her body was laid to rest in Woodlawn cemetery Sunday afternoon at 3 o'clock.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides her immediate family, Mrs Moore is survived by two brothers [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-Lanning/6000000202121422870?through=6000000202121137911 Theodore Lanning], Dennison merchant, and [https://www.geni.com/peop
2 CONC le/John-Lanning/6000000202121634825?through=6000000202121422870 John H. Lanning] of Tippecanoe.  ❞
2 CONT
2 CONT 5em>— 📰 ''The Evening Chronicle (Uhrichville, Ohio) · Weds, Oct 22, 1919'' {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000224833545940&size=large}} 
2 CONT 5em>''Clipping saved by son, Earl T. Moore''
2 CONT
